    "result_text": "`BUILD SUCCESS` \u2014 the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` opt-out already existed in the create pipeline (`RequireUniqueHousehold` skips the duplicate-household 409 when `sharesHousehold` is true), so joiners at an address already used by a same-lastName owner are already *allowed*. What was missing was the **shared, stable `householdId`** returned on the owner.\n\nI implemented it as a **derived value**, keeping structural impact minimal \u2014 **2 files, no changes to the `Owner` entity, DB schema, or the pipeline** (all high-context / high-WMC surfaces):\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` string to the `Owner` response schema (generates `OwnerDto.householdId`).\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one new `@Mapping` line on `toOwnerDto`, plus two tiny cohesive helpers:\n   - `householdId(owner)` \u2192 `String.format(\"H-%08X\", key.hashCode())` over the normalized `lastName|address` key (CC 1).\n   - `normalizeHousehold(value)` \u2192 same trim/collapse-whitespace/lowercase normalization the duplicate-household guard uses (CC 2).\n\nBecause the id is derived deterministically from the normalized last name + address \u2014 the exact key the guard uses to decide two owners share a household \u2014 any two household members compute an **identical, stable** `householdId` across independent GET requests, with no need to mutate or re-save the existing owner. The test `coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` (B and C sharing A's lastName+address with `sharesHousehold=true`) now reads back matching, non-blank `householdId` values.\n\nAdding a field to the response is backward-compatible \u2014 no existing test asserts a strict owner-JSON shape, and only the acceptance test references `householdId`.",
